## 0. NON-NEGOTIABLE INSTRUCTION TO ANTIGRAVITY

Treat the existing code as the **visual and behavioral source of truth**.

The migration is successful only if:

1. Every existing department screen remains available.
2. Existing UI text, labels, government identity, information hierarchy, tables, drawers, filters, buttons, badges, chips, modal behavior, navigation relationships, and prototype interactions are preserved unless this document explicitly changes implementation architecture.
3. The application is converted from Vite SPA architecture into Next.js App Router architecture.
4. The application becomes installable as a PWA.
5. Authenticated/sensitive government workflow data is handled safely; the service worker must not indiscriminately cache sensitive API responses or mutation requests.
6. The existing giant `src/App.tsx` is decomposed into maintainable route, feature, component, data, and type files.
7. URL navigation replaces the current large in-memory `activeDeptItem` / `appSubPage` routing state.
8. No screen is deleted because it looks repetitive.
9. No content is silently replaced by generic placeholder UI.
10. No new product feature should be invented merely to make the migration easier.
11. Existing sample data may stay as fixture/mock data until backend APIs are connected, but it must be moved out of UI components.
12. Do not rename business concepts such as Business DNA, Regulatory Dependency, Delta Re-scrutiny, Query Builder, Inspection Workspace, Decision Record, SLA, Grievance, Regulatory Assistant, etc. unless a separate product decision instructs it.
13. Do not convert the portal into a generic dashboard template.
14. Do not remove GIGW/accessibility-oriented controls merely because they are prototype controls.
15. Build and type-check must remain green at the end of every migration phase.
16. Canonical IDs and state dictionaries must be locked before migrating M06+; do not let each route invent its own `status: string` model.
17. UI labels such as **Under Review** are presentation text; they must not silently replace the canonical backend/domain state.
18. The Department app must use the same underlying application/query/inspection/decision/dependency identities expected by the Entrepreneur app and shared EKATMA backend.
19. MIDC is the first Department Pack, **not** the architecture. Do not copy MIDC-specific code to create future MPCB/Fire/DISH/Boiler/Legal Metrology portals.
20. RAG may retrieve/explain regulations, but it must not own or mutate application state, regulatory applicability, dependency unlocking, or department decisions.
21. Production must remain server-capable; do not convert the authenticated Department Portal into a static-export-only PWA.
22. Figma-generated specification/reference material must remain traceable until parity is proven; do not silently delete it during migration.

### Golden Rule

**Do not rebuild the product from memory. Migrate the code that exists.**

