## Runtime and version policy

At migration time:

1. Use **Node.js 20.9 or newer**; this is the documented minimum for the current Next.js line. Record the actual Node and pnpm versions used for the migration.
2. Install the **latest patched Active LTS** release of Next.js. At the time this document was authored/re-verified, the official Next.js docs displayed version 16.3.6.
3. Do not pin to an obsolete version copied from an old tutorial.
4. Keep React / React DOM on versions compatible with the chosen Next.js release.
5. Run `pnpm audit` and review advisories before final deployment. Do not apply blind major-version `--force` upgrades solely to silence audit output.
6. Commit the resulting `pnpm-lock.yaml`; the lockfile is part of the reproducible migration state.

## THIS repository must be converted in place

**Do not run `create-next-app` in a way that creates `Bianca Dept/ekatma-department/` or any other nested second application.** The existing repository is the target. The current Vite source is the reference to be decomposed.

For this repository, update dependencies/configuration in place. A current baseline is conceptually:

```bash
# Resolve/install the current patched Next.js Active LTS and its compatible React versions.
pnpm add next@latest react@latest react-dom@latest

# Tailwind v4 with Next.js uses PostCSS, not the Vite Tailwind plugin.
pnpm add -D @tailwindcss/postcss postcss eslint eslint-config-next

# Remove Vite-only runtime/build integrations only after the Next.js equivalent works.
pnpm remove vite @vitejs/plugin-react @tailwindcss/vite
```

If the newest `next@latest` has moved to a new major by the time this guide is executed, first verify the then-current Active LTS and compatibility before changing majors. The objective is a patched supported baseline, not an unnecessary framework upgrade experiment.

Create `postcss.config.mjs` with the Tailwind v4 PostCSS plugin:

```js
const config = {
  plugins: {
    '@tailwindcss/postcss': {},
  },
}

export default config
```

Retain `@import "tailwindcss";` and the existing CSS-first `@theme` tokens when moving `src/index.css` to `src/app/globals.css`.

