Never trust a client-side hidden/disabled button as authorization.

### Login CAPTCHA migration rule

The current prototype CAPTCHA is client-generated and is appropriate only as a visual prototype. During migration:

- do not treat a browser-generated CAPTCHA code as a production security control
- production CAPTCHA/challenge validation must be server/backend issued and validated
- avoid generating `Math.random()`, timestamps, or locale-dependent values during the initial server/client render when they can cause hydration mismatch
- if temporary visual parity requires the prototype CAPTCHA before backend integration, initialize it after hydration or pass a stable initial value from the server boundary; mark it clearly as mock/prototype behavior

### Proxy is not the authorization system

If `src/proxy.ts` is used, use it for coarse request-time redirects/checks only. Every protected data read and mutation still requires server-side authorization. In Next.js 16, use `proxy.ts`; do not introduce the older `middleware.ts` convention alongside it.

